## Why This Is Asked

Introduces feature flags and cache strategies with safe rollout and rollback, a practical skill for production CNPA systems.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and governance
- Read-through cache pattern with Redis
- Cache invalidation/write-through on updates
- Canary rollout and observable rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: flag check and cached read
function getEndpointData(endpoint){
  if (config.getFlag('cache:' + endpoint)){
    const v = cache.get(endpoint)
    if (v) return v
    const data = db.query(endpoint)
    cache.set(endpoint, data, TTL)
    return data
  }
  return db.query(endpoint)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cache invalidation under concurrent writes?
- How would you handle a cache stampede or Redis outage?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces access control in a CNPA stack, a common beginner-friendly area with real-world implications.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC, middleware, JWT validation
- Log redaction and PII protection
- Unit/integration tests, basic load testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Express-style middleware sketch
function rbac(requiredRoles){
  return (req,res,next)=>{
    const token=req.headers.authorization?.split(' ')[1];
    const payload=verifyJwt(token); // assume helper
    if(!payload || !requiredRoles.includes(payload.role)) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
    req.user=payload; next();
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support multi-tenant roles?
- How would you test for token revocation and short-lived tokens?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of practical, beginner-friendly schema evolution across HTTP input, DB writes, and Kafka events without breaking existing consumers.

## Key Concepts

- Backward-compatible schema evolution
- Optional/additive fields and defaults
- Schema registry with compatibility levels
- Canary rollout and feature flags
- Contract and end-to-end testing

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    id: { type: "string" },
    event: { type: "string" },
    customer_segment: { type: "string" }
  },
  required: ["id", "event"],
  additionalProperties: true
};
function validate(payload){
  const valid = ajv.validate(schema, payload);
  if(!valid) throw new Error(ajv.errors.map(e=>e.message).join(", "));
  return payload;
}
```

